The first use is a SELECT: only the base column is returned, the generated column is not expanded:
a
10
The generated column is still readable when named explicitly:
10	11
Explicitly writing into the generated column is rejected:
Cannot insert column b, because it is MATERIALIZED column
The first use is an INSERT without a column list: only the base column is written, SQLite computes the generated column:
a	b
5	6
10	11
